-# IP Address Manager
+# IP Address Manager (full stack)
 
-Track which device uses which IP address. Work in progress.
+Track which device uses which IP address, detect conflicts, and see free addresses on a subnet map.
+
+- **Frontend:** React + Vite (`client/`)
+- **Backend:** Node.js REST API (`server/`), built-in `http` and `node:sqlite`, no npm packages needed
+- **Database:** SQLite file created automatically at `server/data.db`
+
+## Requirements
+Node.js **22.13 or newer** (`node -v` to check). Node 22 prints an "experimental" warning for SQLite. That is normal.
+
+## Run it (two terminals)
+
+    # terminal 1: API on http://localhost:3001
+    cd server
+    npm start
+
+    # terminal 2: React app on http://localhost:5173
+    cd client
+    npm install
+    npm run dev
+
+Open http://localhost:5173.
+
+## Run the tests
+
+    cd server
+    npm test
+
+## API
+
+| Method | Path | What it does |
+|---|---|---|
+| GET | /api/devices | List all devices, sorted by IP |
+| POST | /api/devices | Add a device (409 if the IP is taken, with a free suggestion) |
+| PUT | /api/devices/:id | Update a device |
+| DELETE | /api/devices/:id | Delete a device |
+| GET | /api/subnet?cidr=192.168.1.0/24 | Used and free addresses for a subnet (up to /22) |
+| GET | /api/types | Allowed device types |
+
+## How it works
+1. The form sends JSON to the API. The server validates it (`validate.js`) and rejects bad IPs.
+2. IPs are stored as 32-bit numbers (`ip.js`), so sorting and range queries are simple.
+3. If the IP already exists, the API answers 409 and suggests the next free address in that /24.
+4. The subnet map asks `/api/subnet` and draws one square per address. Tap a free square to fill the form.
+
+## Ideas to extend
+Login with roles (admin/viewer), CSV export, a VLAN field, a ping check for online status.
